      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Alkaline environments significantly accelerate the Maillard reaction because the hydroxide ions shift the equilibrium toward the deprotonated, more nucleophilic form of the amino group, which attacks the sugar carbonyl faster. Conversely, acidic conditions suppress this nucleophilicity by protonating the amino group, effectively creating a barrier to the initial binding step and slowing down browning. While a higher pH also promotes the retro-aldol and Strecker degradation of intermediates after the initial attack, the overall rate increases dramatically with alkalinity due to the enhanced reactivity of the amino nucleophile.",
      "source": "target",
